  "id": "COMPOUND:14575 - PROTEIN:6231",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that pioglitazone may interact with Histone-lysine N-methyltransferase, H3 lysine-9 specific 5 through a series of molecular targets. Pioglitazone is directly linked to the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex, which is a known target of multiple compounds, including (+)-Phylliroside, quillaic acid, and Dimethyl 3-hydroxyphthalate. These compounds are all capable of influencing the activity of the histone methyltransferase complex, which plays a critical role in chromatin modification and gene regulation. The consistent presence of this relationship across three distinct pathways, each involving different but related molecular targets, indicates a potential mechanism by which pioglitazone modulates the activity of the histone methyltransferase complex. The evidence supports the idea that pioglitazone may exert its biological effects through interactions with this complex, although the exact nature of the interaction and its functional significance remain to be fully elucidated.",
